  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)={(1-3x)}^{8},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)=1-3x$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{8}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{8})^{′}=8{g}^{7}, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =(1-3x)^{′}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =8{g}^{7}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-24(1-3x)^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
